      "documentConcept": "AI and machine learning in PD diagnosis and progression",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:3cfd6c0e8e0beab503a401f8411f031e70b9c222b7a09384411b472724225cd7",
      "text": "Systematic reviews of machine learning in Parkinson's disease report a translation gap: very few systems have been adopted for real-world use, external validation is uncommon and accuracy is lower on external test sets, and data leakage is associated with inflated accuracy. The reviews also report high headline accuracy alongside low-quality evidence and a high risk of bias in most assessed studies, and a preprint reports that subject-aware splitting lowers reported performance. A machine learning study of longitudinal clinical data reports projections of progression and a replication in an independent cohort. The reviews' proportions describe the samples assessed, not the whole literature.",
